- Debt: Generally, higher debt might indicate lower credit scoring due to higher financial burden; lower debt is favorable.  
- Overdue days: More overdue days usually correlate with poor credit scoring; fewer or zero overdue days indicate better credit scoring.  
- Initial limit: A higher initial credit limit might be assigned to clients with higher credit scoring; a low initial limit may indicate lower creditworthiness.  
- BIRTHDATE: Age can influence credit scoring; middle-aged clients tend to have more stable credit history than very young or very old clients.  
- SEX: Gender itself may have a minor or no direct impact, but sometimes lending patterns differ.  
- EDU (Education): Higher education (University, College) is often associated with better credit scoring due to better income prospects.  
- INCOME: Higher income generally correlates with higher credit scoring.  
- TERM: Longer credit terms might be riskier, so shorter terms might be associated with higher credit scoring.  
- Credit history rating: This is a direct indicator; better ratings (like A1, A2, A3) correspond to higher credit scoring, worse ratings (D1, E1, etc.) correspond to lower scoring.  
- LV_AREA: Certain regions might have better average credit scoring due to economic factors.  
- LV_SETTLEMENTNAME: Some settlements or cities might have clients with better or worse credit scoring on average.  
- INDUSTRYNAME: Some industries (Finance, IT) might have more stable income and better credit scoring; others (Agriculture, Construction) might be riskier.  
- PDN (probability of default number): Higher PDN indicates lower credit scoring; lower PDN indicates higher credit scoring.  
- UNDERAGECHILDRENCOUNT: More dependents might increase financial burden, possibly lowering credit scoring.  
- FAMILYSTATUS: Married or stable family status might positively influence credit scoring due to perceived stability.  

